I recently inherited a 18-year-old portable (an Atari 'stacy')
from its previous owner.  It hadn't been turned on, she told
me, in 14 years. It's a clunky, 18-pound machine, with the
approximate profile of a portable electric typewriter and a
monochrome LCD screen of low resolution.

I had assumed it was junk;  I had planned to take it apart
to get the trackball and tiny keyswitches (it has a funky
keyboard) for another project.

But, out of a spirit of experimentation, I plugged it in and
switched it on.

Imagine my surprise.  It works perfectly, and the 30Mbyte hard
drive is still readable.  It took a long time to boot up the
first time, and I got lots of messages about filesystem nodes
being repaired from hamming codes, but the data appears to be
all intact. It has a 2Mbyte memory, Atari OS in ROM, the Neodesk
windowing system (think mac system 3 for Atari) a Motorola
MC68000 CPU, the "killer apps" of 1987 to 1990 (wordperfect,
pageflow, several others), viewers for several graphic formats,
no network code whatsoever (whoever heard of the web in 1989?),
a few games including reversi, and ....

Nethack.

Nethack 3.0, for Atari, with a build date of February 1989.

And it works.

I started up a Rogue, played it to second level but then
died to a goblin with a wand of somethingorother. And then
I sat there giggling for a few minutes; I've just been
handed the ultimate dream system for a retrocomputist
who's into roguelikes!

With a little more experimentation, and a bit of work
figuring out the different OS, I plan to poke around the
HD and see if the source code is on it.

